  :adr/body "## What was asked

Owner instruction, 2026-09-07: 描画中も 追加入力, /steer, /queue などを入力したい.

## What made it impossible

A run owned the terminal for its whole duration. The block was erased when the
turn started and drawn again when it ended, and the only key that meant
anything in between was ESC, because the only listener attached in between
watched for ESC.

So `/steer` -- a command whose entire purpose is to reach a run that is ALREADY
GOING -- could not be typed while one was. It had been shipped in that state,
and its own docstring said what it was for.

## The change

The input loop and the turn are now separate things.

The loop runs from the moment the REPL opens until the process exits: ONE stdin
listener for the life of the session, where there used to be one per turn,
attached when a line was wanted and removed when it arrived. A turn is state
the loop reads, not a thing that takes the keyboard away.

On submit:

- **a slash command runs immediately**, whatever the turn is doing. That is the
  point.
- **a plain line starts a turn** if none is in flight, and **joins the queue**
  if one is. Two turns at once against one profile is two conversations, and
  the server would interleave them.
- the queue drains when a turn completes, one at a time.

`/queue` lists what is waiting; `/queue clear` discards it. `/steer` and
`/stop` now read the run id from the turn in flight -- they read it from the
HELD-CARD state before, so they only worked when a write was waiting for
approval, which is not when anyone wants to steer.

## The screen, when two things want it

Everything that writes during the REPL goes through one function, `say!`, which
erases the block, writes, and schedules the block to come back. `*print-fn*` is
rebound onto it, so every `println` in every slash command lands above the
frame without any of them knowing the frame exists. A write that skips it lands
on top of the frame, and the frame is what the next erase is measured from.

Three things were found by measuring this, each of which had a plausible
implementation that was wrong:

**nbb hands `*print-fn*` a line WITHOUT its newline.** So every slash command
ended mid-row and the block was drawn over it: `/queue` printed a list nobody
ever saw. The newline is added at the rebinding.

**Breaking the line before drawing moved the block down a row per redraw.**
Arriving from raw output the cursor really is on a half-written row and the
line has to be broken -- but `erase-input!` already leaves the cursor on the
row BELOW it, so doing it again there walked the block down the screen. Eight
blank rows between the answer and the frame, measured. It is done only when
arriving from raw output.

**Returning to a partial line by cursor arithmetic does not work.** A partial
line wider than the terminal wraps, and `ESC[nC` clamps at the last column, so
a streamed line of sixty numbers resumed at the far right of the wrong row.

That last one is why the answer's incomplete last line is now a ROW OF THE
BLOCK rather than something left on the terminal: inside the block the row
count is already true, and the line graduates to the scrollback the moment its
newline arrives. The progress line moved for the same reason -- both used to be
lines of their own, which means being told where the block is and disagreeing
with it the moment either moves.

The block is therefore: the answer's tail, the progress line, the rule, the
input, the rule, the status bar. The tail comes first because it continues the
answer directly above it, and a progress line between them would cut the
sentence in half.

## A refusal ends the turn, not the session

`itonami chat \"…\"` keeps `exit 1` on a refusal: that is where a status has to
be something a script can read. In the REPL it now prints and returns to the
prompt.

The reason is the same one that made ESC return to the prompt rather than exit:
an operator who typed one thing the server would not take has not asked to
leave, and with a queue behind it, leaving would take the queue too. A refused
turn leaves the queue unsent and says so, with the two commands that read and
discard it.

Measured while writing this: `この Bot はすでに実行中です` from a leftover run
ended the whole session. It now prints and the prompt comes back.

## Measured

On a pty of known size, against the live resident:

- `/status` and `/queue` answer while a turn streams; their output lands above
  the frame and the frame comes back under it.
- Typing during a run works, Japanese included; Enter queues, the status bar
  shows `⏸ 1 件待機 (/queue)`, and the queue drains into the next turn when the
  first completes (`say A` streaming, `say B` typed and queued, `say B` sent).
- `/steer` twenty seconds into a sixty-number count reaches the run: it ends
  with `DONE`.
- A sixty-number answer wider than the terminal wraps in the scrollback and its
  tail sits in the block without either overwriting the other.

`/steer` in the first seconds of a turn still reports no run, because until the
POST returns there is no run id -- there is genuinely nothing to steer yet.
"}]
